    "discipline": "Industrial",
    "subject": "Engineering Economics",
    "chapter": "Evaluation of Alternatives",
    "topic": "Internal Rate of Return",
    "subtopic": "Overview",
    "content_html": "<ul>\r\n<li>The discounted rate that equates the present value of a project's expected cash inflows to the present value of the project's costs.</li>\r\n\r\n<li>Higher a project's internal rate of return, the more desirable it is to undertake the project.</li>\r\n\r\n<li>Internal rate of return (IRR) is also sometimes referred as Economic Rate of Return.</li>\r\n\r\n<li><b>Internal</b> refers to the fact that its calculation does not incorporate external environmental factors such as interest rate or inflation.</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n\r\n\r\n<h5><b>Definition:</b></h5>\r\n<p>The Internal Rate of Return (IRR) is a financial metric used to evaluate the attractiveness or profitability of an investment or project. It represents the annualized rate of return that an investment is expected to generate over its lifetime.</p>\r\n\r\n<h5><b>Calculation:</b></h5>\r\n<p>The IRR is the discount rate at which the net present value (NPV) of cash flows from an investment becomes zero. In other words, it's the rate that equates the sum of the present values of cash inflows and outflows.\r\n\r\nMathematically, the IRR is found by solving the following equation for \"r\" (the IRR):\r\n\r\n\\[0 = \\sum \\dfrac{C_t}{(1 + r)^t}\\]\r\n\r\nWhere:\r\n- \\(C_t\\) represents the cash flow at time \"t.\"<br/>\r\n- \"r\" is the IRR (the rate we want to find).<br/>\r\n- \"t\" represents different time periods.</p>\r\n\r\n<h5><b>Interpretation:</b></h5>\r\n<ol>\r\n<li>Positive IRR: If the calculated IRR is positive, it indicates that the investment is expected to generate returns greater than the required rate of return (the discount rate used). In this case, the project is considered economically viable.</li>\r\n\r\n<li>Negative IRR: A negative IRR suggests that the investment is not expected to generate sufficient returns to cover the required rate of return. In such cases, the project is generally considered unattractive.</li>\r\n\r\n<li>IRR = Discount Rate:** When the IRR equals the discount rate (the rate used to discount future cash flows), the NPV of the investment is zero. This means that the investment is expected to break even, and it is often used as a benchmark for investment decisions.</li>\r\n</ol>",
    "important": "\\[0 = \\sum \\dfrac{C_t}{(1 + r)^t}\\]",
    "resources": "",
    "images": [],
    "local_images": [],
    "video": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1kF4LcCx4cH9Yp5MtO1nnNudqFulqr27n/preview",
    "videoTitle": "Internal Rate of Return"
