    "discipline": "Industrial",
    "subject": "Quality",
    "chapter": "Quality Management, Planning, Assurance, and Systems",
    "topic": "Fishbone",
    "subtopic": "Overview",
    "content_html": "<div class=\"container-fluid\">\r\n\t<div class=\"row align-items-start\">\r\n\t\t<div class=\"col col-lg-8\">\r\n<p>The fishbone diagram shows numerous potential root causes of an impact or issue. A brainstorming session can be organized using it. Ideas are categorised instantly into practical groups.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Fishbone diagram is sometimes also referred as cause-and-effect diagram or Ishikawa diagram.</p>\r\n\r\n\t\t</div>\r\n    \t\t<div class=\"col\">\r\n     <center><img src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/52/Ishikawa_Fishbone_Diagram.svg\" alt=\"Ishikawa Fishbone Diagram\" style=\"max-width: 50vh;\"><br/>\r\n<small><small><a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ishikawa_Fishbone_Diagram.svg\">FabianLange at de.wikipedia</a>, <a href=\"http://www.gnu.org/copyleft/fdl.html\">GFDL</a>, via Wikimedia Commons</small></small></center><br/>\r\n\r\n    \t\t</div>\r\n  \t</div>\r\n</div>",
